# The golang-go floor below follows go.mod's toolchain line — the Go every
# release is built and tested with — not the oldest Go that compiles the source.
# That is the go directive, and GOTOOLCHAIN=local holds a build to it alone:
# measured, go1.26.8 builds both binaries and go1.25.14 refuses. It said >= 1.21
# since the 2.0.0 rewrite, which was false — the web process calls
# http.NewCrossOriginProtection, a Go 1.25 API. Debian trixie ships 1.24, so
# building this package there needs golang from backports.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), golang-go (>= 2:1.27~)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://easywall-project.org
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/jp1337/easywall
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/jp1337/easywall.git
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: easywall
Architecture: any
# ca-certificates is a hard dependency: without the bundle every HTTPS request
# easywall makes — update check, feeds, country lists, ACME, notifications —
# fails certificate verification, and --no-install-recommends leaves it out.
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, adduser, ca-certificates, nftables, openssl
Recommends: logrotate
Description: Linux firewall management with a web interface
 easywall provides a secure web interface for managing nftables firewall rules
 on Linux. It uses a two-process architecture: a privileged core daemon
 communicates with the kernel via netlink, while an unprivileged web process
 serves the UI. Communication happens over a Unix socket.
 .
 Features include TCP/UDP port management, IP blocklists and allowlists,
 port forwarding, custom nftables rules, protection against SSH brute-force,
 SYN floods, ICMP floods, port scans, and more.
 .
 A two-step activation mechanism prevents accidental lockouts: rules can be
 automatically rolled back if connectivity is not confirmed.
